WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.880
Zombies have been an essential presence in&nbsp;
film ever since "Night of the Living Dead"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:10.400
released in 1968, but they've found serious&nbsp;
mainstream appeal in the past several decades.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:15.920
Here are some 21st century masterpieces that&nbsp;
fans of all things undead can't afford to miss.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:22.000
Before he got his handful of Oscars for "Slumdog&nbsp;
Millionaire," British director Danny Boyle grabbed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:28.640
audiences' attention with 2002's "28 Days Later,"&nbsp;
which stars Cillian Murphy as a man who awakens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:33.760
from a coma and finds himself in an abandoned&nbsp;
London. Both the protagonist and the audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.720
are thrust right into a world turned upside down,&nbsp;
one that's full of violent monsters that have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:43.840
infected with the aptly named Rage Virus. Even&nbsp;
more frightening than the relentless zombies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.560
however, are the greedy humans who find&nbsp;
themselves going mad with their newfound power.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:54.080
Although Boyle would go on to direct slow-burning&nbsp;
dramas based on real life like "127 Hours" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:59.600
"Steve Jobs," "28 Days Later" showed off his&nbsp;
ability to direct grisly horror at breakneck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:04.800
speed. Grimy yet intimate cinematography and&nbsp;
emotional supporting performances from Naomie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.760
Harris and Brendan Gleeson make for an experience&nbsp;
that's as raw as the meat that zombies love to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:14.080
consume. Even considering all of the other&nbsp;
impressive movies Boyle has helmed in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:19.200
decades since its release, "28 Days Later"&nbsp;
still remains one of his finest creations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:24.320
as well as an essential and influential film&nbsp;
in the 21st-century canon of zombie cinema.

00:01:25.513 --> 00:01:29.120
"Shaun of the Dead" may not be the first&nbsp;
film to lovingly parody zombie cinema,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:33.360
but it's certainly one of the funniest. Edgar&nbsp;
Wright's second feature film was his first to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:38.480
feature the dynamic duo of Simon Pegg and Nick&nbsp;
Frost, a combination that has proven its worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:43.280
time and time again with projects like the&nbsp;
movie "Hot Fuzz" and the TV show "Spaced."

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:48.080
This hilarious story about the last people to&nbsp;
learn about the zombie apocalypse showcases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:53.040
Wright's British sense of humor, impeccable&nbsp;
comedic timing, and trademark energy behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:57.760
the camera. Filled with quotable lines and&nbsp;
recurring gags, the banter between Shaun and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:03.040
his friends is a breath of fresh air in a genre&nbsp;
so often filled with nothing but doom and gloom.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:07.840
"What's the matter, David?&nbsp;
Not taken a shortcut before?"

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.920
Charming critics and audiences alike,&nbsp;
"Shaun of the Dead" proved itself to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:19.280
a 21st century comedy classic. Not only did&nbsp;
it kick off the iconic "Cornetto Trilogy"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:23.040
that continued with "Hot Fuzz" and "The&nbsp;
World's End," but it also established&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:27.440
Wright as a filmmaker to watch and set a&nbsp;
new standard for not just zombie comedies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:30.960
but for comedies in general. Not&nbsp;
too shabby for a rookie director.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:36.320
In the wake of "Twilight" making serious cash&nbsp;
at the box office, it was only a matter of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:41.360
time before more human-monster romances made&nbsp;
their way to the silver screen. One of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:46.960
films was 2013's "Warm Bodies," which swapped&nbsp;
out sparkling vampires for shambling zombies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:51.440
However, viewers expecting just another&nbsp;
"Twilight" knock-off were in for a surprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:56.160
as director Jonathan Levine managed to imbue&nbsp;
the film with more heart and soul than anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.200
expected a movie about reanimated corpses to have.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:03.440
Rather than delivering what might be considered&nbsp;
"realistic" visions of how the infected might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:07.840
look and act, "Warm Bodies" revels in the&nbsp;
awkward oddity of classic zombies — the ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:12.800
that eat brains, move slow, and can barely&nbsp;
speak. This makes for plenty of fun comedy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:17.280
and some unexpectedly heartfelt moments when&nbsp;
a walking corpse falls head over rotting heels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.440
for a human woman after defending her&nbsp;
from several of his undead compatriots.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:26.800
Totally committed lead performances from Nicholas&nbsp;
Hoult and Teresa Palmer help deliver a movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:31.360
romance that's more than a little off the beaten&nbsp;
path; a relationship that you can't help but root&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:36.400
for. This Romeo and Juliet story is filled with&nbsp;
heart, whether that heart is beating or not.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:40.720
In the late 2000s, nearly a decade&nbsp;
after "The Blair Witch Project"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:45.680
brought found-footage into the mainstream,&nbsp;
the genre saw a major resurgence with films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:49.760
like the low-budget supernatural thriller&nbsp;
"Paranormal Activity" and the mysterious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.240
kaiju feature "Cloverfield." This trend&nbsp;
wasn't limited just to America, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:59.200
as Spain delivered its own unique spin on the&nbsp;
zombie genre with the incredibly freaky "REC."

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:03.520
The film follows a reporter who accompanies&nbsp;
a group of firemen to an emergency at an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:07.920
apartment building where a zombie outbreak&nbsp;
has recently begun, only to find themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:14.080
locked inside by the authorities with nowhere to&nbsp;
escape. Directors Jaume Balagueró and Paco Plaza&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:19.520
use the found footage format not just as a way to&nbsp;
keep production costs down, but rather as a way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:24.640
immerse audiences in a world of frightening&nbsp;
uncertainty. The brazenly chilling imagery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:29.120
throughout "REC" ensured that it couldn't be&nbsp;
mistaken for any other zombie flick at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.160
embedding itself in viewers'&nbsp;
minds like an axe to the head.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.960
The box office success of "REC" spawned not&nbsp;
only a sequel, but an American remake in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:42.080
form of the 2008 motion picture "Quarantine,"&nbsp;
However, "Quarantine" impressed far fewer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:46.880
critics than the first Spanish film, and more&nbsp;
than a decade later, it's still the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:51.600
"REC" that's keeping moviegoers up at night with&nbsp;
its bold approach to found-footage filmmaking.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:56.640
It's easy to imagine a version of "Zombieland"&nbsp;
that's just an American remake of "Shaun of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:01.760
the Dead," but this 2009 comedy feature&nbsp;
was able to establish its own identity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:06.560
on the backs of its many iconic performances.&nbsp;
Jesse Eisenberg's neurotic everyman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:10.240
Woody Harrelson's thrill-seeking&nbsp;
badass, and Emma Stone and Abigail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:15.040
Breslin's wisecracking con artist sisters&nbsp;
make up an eccentric zombie survival squad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:18.800
that's as good at busting guts as they&nbsp;
are at busting hordes of the undead.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:24.160
Director Ruben Fleischer gives "Zombieland" plenty&nbsp;
of unique flourishes that make it stand out in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:29.440
increasingly crowded world of zombie cinema. These&nbsp;
include memorable rules for survival, a colorful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:34.640
climax in an amusement park, and of course, an&nbsp;
unforgettable cameo from Bill Murray as himself.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:37.840
"So do you have any regrets?"

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:40.880
"...Garfield, maybe."

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:44.640
With this many laughs, it's no wonder&nbsp;
that "Zombieland" cultivated a fanbase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:49.200
so devoted that it got a sequel an entire&nbsp;
decade after its release in "Zombieland:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:53.280
Double Tap." Much like the walking&nbsp;
dead, "Zombieland" refuses to die,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:58.160
and it's not hard to see why: comedies this&nbsp;
good are rarer than a Twinkie in the apocalypse.

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:02.640
Looking for a zombie movie to watch with&nbsp;
the whole family? The options may be scarce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:06.560
but "ParaNorman" proves they're out&nbsp;
there. The second film from Laika Studios,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:11.520
"ParaNorman" is the offbeat story of Norman, a&nbsp;
kid who can see ghosts, and follows his quest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:16.640
to save his small town from Puritan zombies and a&nbsp;
witch's curse. The project may not be as intense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:21.520
as your typical zombie flick, but it shouldn't&nbsp;
surprise anyone familiar with Laika's infamously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:26.960
creepy first feature, "Coraline," that there's no&nbsp;
shortage of scares here, regardless of age rating.

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:30.640
The zombies and ghosts in "ParaNorman"&nbsp;
aren't just here for the frights, though;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:34.800
they help tell a delightful story about&nbsp;
friendship, acceptance, and what makes all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:40.080
of us unique. Each frame is lovingly crafted&nbsp;
with Laika's impressive stop-motion animation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:45.120
which lends a signature tactile quality to&nbsp;
everything from a zombie's dangling jaw to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:50.000
smear of nail polish. "ParaNorman" is a film&nbsp;
that's equal parts chilling and thoughtful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:54.320
and its overwhelming heart has made it one of&nbsp;
the more critically acclaimed animated movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:59.280
of the 21st century. This insightful take&nbsp;
on the genre is perfect for horror-loving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:02.560
parents who want to get their children&nbsp;
in on the action from a young age.

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:07.520
Zombie movies have been used as a means for&nbsp;
providing social commentary since "Night of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:12.640
the Living Dead," a trend that continued into&nbsp;
2020 with Jeff Barnaby's "Blood Quantum."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:17.360
In this film, as the zombie apocalypse spreads&nbsp;
across North America, the native residents of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:22.800
a First Nations reserve find themselves strangely&nbsp;
immune from the infection. Unfortunately for them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:27.600
there's no such thing as immunity from getting&nbsp;
torn to shreds, so three generations must work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:32.720
together to navigate the new undead status quo&nbsp;
and save as many people as they possibly can.

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:37.840
Throughout its runtime, "Blood Quantum" offers&nbsp;
ruminations on experiences specific to indigenous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:42.400
individuals, who rarely find themselves in&nbsp;
heroic leading roles in mainstream film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:46.800
Excellent performances by indigenous&nbsp;
actors Michael Greyeyes, Forrest Goodluck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:51.280
and Stonehorse Lone Goeman make for an&nbsp;
undeniably harrowing viewing experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:55.520
Despite a rocky release and mixed public&nbsp;
reception, "Blood Quantum" received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.520 --> 00:08:00.880
overwhelmingly positive marks from critics,&nbsp;
with Roxana Hadadi from Pajiba praising,

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:05.520
"[It] takes aim at colonization and the&nbsp;
subjugation of native and indigenous peoples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:08.800
and [Barnaby is] wrapping those&nbsp;
considerations in writhing coils&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:12.800
of intestines and splattering them&nbsp;
with a hot burst of arterial blood."

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:16.080
The combination of social&nbsp;
commentary and blood-soaked carnage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:19.280
makes "Blood Quantum" more than&nbsp;
just an entertaining thrill ride.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:23.520
It's also an exciting expansion on the kind&nbsp;
of themes that zombie movies can explore.

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:30.000
"One Cut of the Dead" is, in a word, ingenious.&nbsp;
This Japanese zombie comedy revels in its twists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.520
and turns, but even without spoiling&nbsp;
its big surprises, it's easy to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:39.280
that director Shin'ichirō Ueda displays a level&nbsp;
of creativity never before seen in this genre.

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:43.840
When a crew making a low-budget zombie flick&nbsp;
is attacked by actual, real-life zombies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:48.400
their unhinged director demands that they keep&nbsp;
the camera rolling for maximum authenticity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:52.720
putting everyone around him in mortal danger&nbsp;
in order to get that one perfect shot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:56.160
As actors and crew members fight for&nbsp;
their lives, the camera shows the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:59.840
action in a single extended take,&nbsp;
capturing the chaos in real time.

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:05.040
Ueda does impressive work handling the shift from&nbsp;
a casual hangout vibe to all hell breaking loose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:09.040
all without ever taking a single break in&nbsp;
the action. Keep watching after the credits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:13.440
though, because nothing in this movie is quite&nbsp;
what it seems. At once a rollercoaster ride&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:17.040
and an exercise in unearthing depth&nbsp;
in even the most unexpected places,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:20.124
the sense of creativity in "One Cut&nbsp;
of the Dead" is very much alive!

00:09:21.520 --> 00:09:26.080
A Christmas zombie musical might sound like a&nbsp;
mismatched mess, but "Anna and the Apocalypse"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:30.240
not only proves that it can be done, but&nbsp;
that it can be done well. This yuletide tale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:34.880
following Anna and her high school friends as&nbsp;
they try to navigate a sudden zombie apocalypse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:39.280
works precisely because it leans completely&nbsp;
into everything that makes it unique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:43.360
transporting audiences into a campy&nbsp;
world full of festive frights.

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:47.360
Just because this is a musical doesn't mean the&nbsp;
zombies are any less menacing, though; "Anna and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:52.000
the Apocalypse" doesn't skimp on the blood and&nbsp;
gore, especially when Anna's signature candy cane&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:56.320
weapon is involved. The musical numbers aren't&nbsp;
lacking, either; tunes like "Hollywood Ending"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.320 --> 00:10:00.800
and "Soldier at War" are sure to stay stuck&nbsp;
in your head even long after the credits roll.

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:05.520
"Anna and the Apocalypse" isn't smashing&nbsp;
together genres to make some belabored punchline;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:10.640
its affection for every type of storytelling&nbsp;
it lampoons is both evident and endearing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:14.480
Like so many culinary treats, "Anna and the&nbsp;
Apocalypse" is what happens when you get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:18.080
something magical out of fusing together&nbsp;
things that aren't typically paired up.

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:23.120
"Zombies on a train" might sound as silly an&nbsp;
idea as "Snakes on a Plane," but that's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:27.840
central conceit behind "Train to Busan," one&nbsp;
of the greatest zombie movies not just of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:32.960
21st century, but of all time. Writer&nbsp;
Park Joo-suk and director Yeon Sang-ho&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.960 --> 00:10:37.040
trap a packed cast of characters into the&nbsp;
cramped confines of a train that suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:41.920
becomes infested with hordes of the undead. The&nbsp;
passengers must move beyond barriers of class and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:46.800
age and work as a team in order to survive their&nbsp;
perilous journey to the coastal city of Busan.

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:51.040
While "Train to Busan" will certainly give&nbsp;
any zombie lover their fill of undead carnage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:55.280
it's the humanity of its living protagonists&nbsp;
that truly makes it shine. The characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.280 --> 00:11:00.640
in "Train to Busan" are memorable and lovable —&nbsp;
especially Ma Dong-seok's character Yoon Sang-hwa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:04.480
a charismatic tough guy with a heart&nbsp;
of gold who takes down zombies with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:09.360
fists. With such engaging figures to ground&nbsp;
the story, "Train to Busan" makes audiences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:13.360
cry rather than cheer when its characters&nbsp;
meet their untimely ends. Who knew the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:17.680
key to creating a flawless zombie movie was&nbsp;
simply putting them on public transportation?

